pages.content:
A common pattern in Java is iterating across a specific numerical range defined by **start** and **end** boundary variables.

### Specifying Start and End Boundaries

```java
public class RangeExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int start = 5;
        int end = 10;

        System.out.println("Inclusive range [start, end]:");
        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        System.out.println("Half-open range [start, end):");
        for (int i = start; i < end; i++) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```default
Inclusive range [start, end]:
5 6 7 8 9 10 
Half-open range [start, end):
5 6 7 8 9 
```

### Total Iteration Formulas

- **Inclusive Range (`i <= end`):**
  $$\text{Iterations} = \text{end} - \text{start} + 1$$
  For `start = 5` and `end = 10`: $10 - 5 + 1 = 6$ iterations.

- **Half-Open Range (`i < end`):**
  $$\text{Iterations} = \text{end} - \text{start}$$
  For `start = 5` and `end = 10`: $10 - 5 = 5$ iterations. Half-open ranges are widely used for array and string indexing because valid indices run from `0` to `length - 1`.

