pages.content:
When iterating downwards or counting backwards, use a **negative step range**. This is achieved by decrementing the loop counter with `i--` or `i -= step`.

### Structure of a Countdown Loop

```java
public class CountdownLoop {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Countdown from 5 down to 1
        for (int i = 5; i >= 1; i--) {
            System.out.println("T-minus " + i);
        }
        System.out.println("Liftoff!");
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```default
T-minus 5
T-minus 4
T-minus 3
T-minus 2
T-minus 1
Liftoff!
```

### Common Negative Step Pitfalls

1. **Wrong relational operator:** Using `<` instead of `>`:
   ```java
   for (int i = 10; i <= 1; i--) { // Fails immediately: 10 <= 1 is false!
       System.out.println(i);
   }
   ```
2. **Accidental Infinite Loop:** Decrementing with an upward condition:
   ```java
   for (int i = 10; i >= 1; i++) { // Infinite loop! i keeps increasing, always >= 1
       System.out.println(i);
   }
   ```
3. **Stepping down by multiple units:**
   ```java
   for (int i = 20; i >= 0; i -= 5) {
       System.out.print(i + " "); // Prints: 20 15 10 5 0
   }
   ```

