pages.content:
The `break` statement in Java is used to exit a loop prematurely before the loop termination condition becomes `false`.

### Unlabeled Break

When an unlabeled `break` statement is encountered, Java immediately exits the **innermost loop** enclosing it and resumes execution at the first statement following that loop.

```java
public class BreakDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Search for the first multiple of 7 after 20
        int target = -1;
        for (int i = 21; i <= 50; i++) {
            if (i % 7 == 0) {
                target = i;
                break; // Stop searching immediately
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Found: " + target); // Found: 21
    }
}
```

### Labeled Break in Nested Loops

By default, `break` only exits the inner loop. To break out of outer loops directly, Java provides **labeled break** statements:

```java
public class LabeledBreakDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        searchMatrix:
        for (int row = 0; row < 5; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < 5; col++) {
                if (row == 2 && col == 3) {
                    System.out.println("Target found at (" + row + ", " + col + "). Terminating entire search.");
                    break searchMatrix; // Exits BOTH loops
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Execution resumed outside nested loops.");
    }
}
```

