pages.content:
In Java, variable scope is strictly block-scoped. The scope of a variable declared inside the header of a `for` loop is confined exclusively to the loop header and its body.

### Header Scope vs Pre-declared Scope

```java
public class ScopeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Case 1: Variable declared in for-header
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            System.out.print(i + " ");
        }
        // System.out.println(i); // COMPILE ERROR: cannot find symbol 'i'

        System.out.println();

        // Case 2: Variable declared outside before the loop
        int count;
        for (count = 0; count < 5; count++) {
            // ...
        }
        System.out.println("Final count outside loop: " + count); // Valid! Prints 5
    }
}
```

### Reusing Variable Names in Sibling Loops

Because a variable declared in the `for` header ceases to exist when the loop terminates, you can safely reuse the same variable name (e.g., `i`) in subsequent sibling loops:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { /* ... */ }
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { /* Valid! New 'i' in separate block scope */ }
```

However, in **nested loops**, the inner loop cannot declare a variable with the same name as the outer loop:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { // COMPILE ERROR: variable i is already defined
    }
}
```

