pages.content:
In Java, a `String` is a sequence of `char` values indexed from `0` to `length() - 1`. Traversing strings with a `for` loop is a core technique for searching, filtering, transformation, and validation.

### Accessing Characters and String Length

Two essential `String` methods are used:
- `str.length()`: Returns the total number of characters in the string.
- `str.charAt(index)`: Returns the character at the specified `0`-based index.

```java
public class StringTraversal {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String message = "Java Code";

        // Forward Traversal
        System.out.print("Forward: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < message.length(); i++) {
            System.out.print(message.charAt(i) + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();

        // Reverse Traversal
        System.out.print("Reverse: ");
        for (int i = message.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            System.out.print(message.charAt(i));
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```default
Forward: J a v a   C o d e 
Reverse: edoC avaJ
```

> [!WARNING]
> **Avoid Off-By-One Errors:**
> Writing `i <= message.length()` will compile cleanly, but will throw `StringIndexOutOfBoundsException` at runtime during the final iteration because the maximum valid index is `length() - 1`.

