pages.content:
Multiplication tables demonstrate how single and nested loops generate structured tabular data.

### Generating a Single Multiplication Table

```java
public class SingleTable {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int num = 8;
        int limit = 10;

        System.out.println("=== Multiplication Table for " + num + " ===");
        for (int i = 1; i <= limit; i++) {
            System.out.printf("%d x %2d = %2d%n", num, i, (num * i));
        }
    }
}
```

### Generating a Full 2D Multiplication Grid (1 to 10)

Using nested loops, we can print a complete $10 \times 10$ multiplication table with clean columnar formatting:

```java
public class MultiplicationMatrix {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int size = 10;

        // Print header row
        System.out.print("    ");
        for (int col = 1; col <= size; col++) {
            System.out.printf("%4d", col);
        }
        System.out.println("\n    " + "----".repeat(size));

        // Print rows
        for (int row = 1; row <= size; row++) {
            System.out.printf("%2d |", row);
            for (int col = 1; col <= size; col++) {
                System.out.printf("%4d", (row * col));
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}
```

